; The structural merge, reached through a real map.
;
; paper_adopt and paper_critic both declare an argument `conv` built by the
; premise-less rule `community_convention(accuracy, glue)`. A leaf-free term has
; nothing for qualification to rename, so the two members' terms are equal and
; the linked unit carries ONE argument for them: the composite's `nodes` section
; has two handles, `(paper_adopt conv …)` and `(paper_critic conv …)`, naming the
; same index. That "many handles per one index" shape is what no map could
; produce before, because neither shipped policy has a premise-less rule.
;
; Three things the merge forces are exercised here too: paper_critic's declared
; rebuttals are re-pointed at the argument the merge kept (paper_adopt's, which
; was declared first); the saturation's cross-member pair {paper_adopt,
; paper_critic} against {paper_critic} generates the same two rebuttals, which
; the attack dedupe folds into the transported ones; and paper_adopt's claim,
; justified on its own, is contested in the map, where the objection it never
; saw now attacks the argument it shares.
;
; Committed beside this manifest, regenerated with `lara` (never by hand; see
; scripts/check-map-conformance.sh): map.core.sexp, the parity envelope handed
; to the Lean driver, and map.verdict.sexp, the composite verdict both drivers
; must print.
(lara-map@1
  (policy convention-v1 convention-v1.policy.lara)
  (backends (backend nd 1))
  (members
    (member paper_adopt adopter.lara)
    (member paper_critic critic.lara))
  (alignments
    (alignment
      (ref paper_adopt c_std whole)
      (ref paper_critic c_convention whole)
      same
      (author reviewer_r)
      (audit-status reviewed)
      (rationale "Both papers state the same convention about the same metric and benchmark, which is why their arguments for it are one argument in the map.")))
  (questions))
